course: dictionary
lesson: 6195
title: Guessing wrongly
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To misjudge something is to guess it wrongly. You can misjudge an amount, a distance or a time. The word has mis- in front, which means wrong, and judge, which means to work out or decide.
explain: «A train is faster and closer than it looks, so it is easy to misjudge.» The train looks far away and slow, but it is near and fast. If you guess wrong about how close it is, you misjudge the distance, and that can be dangerous when you cross a road.
explain: You can also misjudge a person. Someone may seem grumpy at first but turn out to be kind. If you form a wrong opinion about them, you have misjudged them. A good check is to swap in "guessed wrongly" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Misjudge means to guess wrongly about something.
fact: You can misjudge an amount, a distance or a time.
fact: To misjudge a person means to form a wrong or unfair opinion about them.
fact: Common phrases are "misjudge the distance" and "misjudge the situation".
fact: Misjudge is a verb: misjudges, misjudged, misjudging.
exercise: «A train is faster and closer than it looks, so it is easy to misjudge.» What does misjudge mean here? | Stop and wait for it to pass | Hear the train clearly | *Guess wrongly about how far or how fast | See the train in the dark
exercise: Ravi thinks the stream is narrow, so he jumps, but he lands in the water. What has he done? | He swam across the stream | He measured the stream exactly | *He misjudged the width of the stream | He made the stream wider
exercise: Mr Patel's class called a boy lazy, but he turned out to be very kind. What did the class do? | They invited him to lunch | They forgot his name | They judged him perfectly | *They misjudged him
test: In a game, Sam misjudged the ball and missed it. What did Sam get wrong? | The name of the game | The score at the end | The colour of the ball | *Where the ball would be
test: What is a good check for the meaning of misjudge in a sentence? | Read only the first word of the sentence | Count the letters in the word | *Swap in "guessed wrongly" and see if it still makes sense | Skip the sentence and move on without thinking
exam: Which sentence uses misjudge correctly? | The runner misjudged the bright, sunny morning and slept | The runner misjudged a happy song | *The runner misjudged the gap and landed in the water | The runner misjudged the sunshine and ate lunch
